Z||000000||Gokart||07-30-99||08:55 PM||||Having owned a Honda, Mazda, and a Toyota myself at one time or another I usually had no problems with them and was quite content. Actually I'm sure I've put more money into the two Fieros I've owned to keep them running than all the other cars combined, but always worth every penny because I loved my Fieros! The only thing that I really dislike about some Japenese cars is that I feel they stole the Fieros design for there own cars and most people will never realize it! To name a few-Del Sol, Pulsar, MR2. Even though they try to come close, they will never get as beautiful as a FIERO!
Z||000001||BN Boomer||07-30-99||10:15 PM||||It's been well known for some time in manufacturing circles that the Japanese rarely come up with anything truly original. They are best at taking other's ideas and refining them, which they have obviously been remarkably successful at.
Z||000002||lowCG||07-30-99||10:46 PM||||Yeah,seems that way,too bad,Japanese art is very interesting,being a farming culture for thousands of years mellows the breed;just that cars are such a Western thing(never had chariots in the East,I don't believe,and we still set the trends.<br>MR2 is a dead ringer for the FIAT X-19,I always thought(1974)which came from the Ghandini show car(1972).<br>Mazda has shared technology with FIAT for some 25 years that I'm aware of,that's the best sharing I've seen,with the best products as a result,imagine this if you will;  a mid engined Mazda,using two of the 1.8liter V6s(V12 arrangement) for power.<br>After WWII,we sent some people over there to set the country up for what has happened(Deming)in the last 50 years,knowing that they had a chance to do something great having a clean canvas to start with.<br>I used to be pretty die-hard eurocar buff,I'm changing though,after all if I can humble myself into a rattly old Fiero,with little bird-insignias all over it,and like it very much,than anything is possible.
