Z||000004||Racer 1||08-02-99||12:20 AM||||I think that the fiero was to way ahead of its time in 1984.And in 84 people liked gas gussling v-8's.And when you drove the 84's iron dukey what a lack of power.Then came the engine fires that scared alot of people away.If GM had waited till 85 to make the fiero and did alittle more R&D by time 1988 gt came around we might a had a 454 v-8 engine in the fiero with 12 inch disks brakes?The MR-2 was always playin catch up with the fiero untill the fiero got the axe..... 8-(
Z||000005||DJRice||08-02-99||07:11 AM||||I still enjoy a good gas-guzzling 5.7L (or more).  But I like my Fiero too.  Fiero was indeed ahead of its time, but If it werent for Fiero, GM would have not been half as succesful with its Saturn line.  Much of the way the car was assembled was used by Saturn, and what about the Dent-Resistant doors? Hmm.<br>My whole car is dent resistant.<p>Pontiacs big problem was women.  Women bought Fieros, and they got tired of the lack of power steering.  Toyota knew it and added an electric PS system to its MR2.<p>I read a pretty good article online about the demise of the Fiero, I'll have to find the link.
Z||000006||ChadMan||08-02-99||08:11 AM||||I remember all the car mags bitching because the Fiero weighed just over 2500 lbs, while competitors like the original MR2 and RX7 were closer to 2000.<p>What we got for our extra 500 lbs was suspensions that don't bend when you hit a big northern pothole, a frame that won't collapse in a 15 mph collision, and body panels that don't rust.  When's the last time you saw an old Japanese sports car up north that didn't look like swiss cheese?
